  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Why Chest Acupressure Points Matter</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">The chest carries the Conception Vessel points CV17 and CV22, together with the Lung points LU1 and LU2, making it a natural focus for breathing and emotional concerns. Shallow, tense breathing is common during stress and anxiety, which is why many people reach for chest acupressure as a complementary self-care practice alongside their usual care. Several points here also appear in our condition guides for coughs, asthma, and anxiety, so the locations you see in this region connect directly to the step-by-step guidance found across the site.</p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">Traditionally, CV17 is described as the point where the chest qi gathers, and CV17, CV22, and the Lung points are used to support smooth breathing and emotional calm. On a physical level, slow pressure on the chest can relax the muscles between the ribs and the upper airway while encouraging slower, fuller breaths. Either way, approach the area carefully, keep pressure gentle, and let breathing stay natural throughout your session.</p>
  </section>

