  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">How to Stimulate Chest Points</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">Chest points sit close to the ribs and breastbone, so press with the flat of one or two fingertips rather than a hard knuckle, and keep the pressure gentle. Use 30 to 60 seconds per point for a quick session, or one to two minutes when you want a deeper feel, working both sides of the body. Keep pressure at roughly 90 degrees to the skin and work around breast tissue rather than directly over it. Seek medical attention if you ever feel chest pain or new shortness of breath instead of pressing.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Can pressing the chest help with anxiety?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">People with anxiety often hold tension in the chest and breathe more shallowly than usual. Gentle pressure on points such as CV17, combined with slow, even breathing, can encourage the body to ease and is a common self-care step used alongside professional care. Acupressure is not a treatment for anxiety disorders, but many people describe it as a calming ritual when breathing feels tight. If anxiety is severe or persistent, speak with a qualified clinician.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Is chest acupressure safe for breathing problems?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">When pressure is light and the point stays comfortable, chest acupressure can be part of self-care for mild respiratory tension, such as a dry tickle or stress-related chest tightness. However, it is not a replacement for medical treatment for breathing conditions. If you experience difficulty breathing, wheezing, chest pain, a persistent high fever, or symptoms that worsen, stop pressing and seek medical attention promptly rather than continuing self-care.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-medium text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How long should I press these points?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">For chest points, 30 to 60 seconds on each side is a sensible starting point, and one to two minutes works well when you want a deeper, more releasing feel. Press both sides evenly at roughly 90 degrees to the skin, aiming for a comfortable deqi response—a deep, dull, slightly aching or full sensation, never sharp. Deepen slow and breathe evenly while you hold, and stop if the point ever feels tender or painful.</div>
    </details>
  </section>

  <div class="mt-10 text-center">
